This is Smale's mean value conjecture (the case K=1), which is explicitly marked `category research open` in the problem statement. It is an open problem in mathematics — no proof is known.

The problem statement itself confirms this:
- `mean_value_problem` (K=1) is labeled `research open`
- Only weaker versions are known: `mean_value_problem_leq_4` (K=4, Smale) and the Tischler results under special root conditions (K=(d-1)/d)

The conjecture asks: given a complex polynomial $p$ of degree $d \ge 2$ and $z \in \mathbb{C}$, there exists a critical point $c$ of $p$ (i.e., $p'(c)=0$) such that

$$\frac{|p(z)-p(c)|}{|z-c|} \le |p'(z)|.$$

This remains unresolved in general. The best unconditional bound is $K=4$ (Smale, 1981), and tighter bounds $K=(d-1)/d$ are known only under special hypotheses (all roots real, or all roots of equal norm) due to Tischler (1989). The case $K=1$ for arbitrary complex polynomials is open.

